# Session cookie flags (httponly, samesite, secure, year-long lifetime) are set in
# PHP by session_boot() in lib/auth.php — NOT here. NearlyFreeSpeech's Apache rejects
# php_flag/php_value in .htaccess with a hard 500 ("directive is not supported; use
# /home/conf/php.ini"), which took the whole suite down. Keep PHP ini out of .htaccess.

# Don't expose server info
ServerSignature Off

# The suite's public name is CalMind, but the path is lowercase and the server's
# filesystem is case-sensitive, so the capitalised forms 404ed. 301 them home. The
# target is rebuilt from REQUEST_URI (never a relative substitution: per-directory
# rewrites prefix those with the *filesystem* path, which leaked the docroot into the
# redirect), so the /test/ and /dev/ mirrors keep their own prefix in %1. The
# all-lowercase form is deliberately not in the alternation — it would rewrite to
# itself and loop.
RewriteEngine On

# ── /CalMind is the canonical spelling (Sean, 2026-08-22) ───────────────────
#
# Served by a SYMLINK on the host, not by a rule here: /home/public/CalMind ->
# calmind, in all three instances. That makes the capitalised path really
# exist, which is what a POST needs — and the POST is the whole point.
#
# The path used to 301 to /calmind, and a 301 is not safely followed by a
# POST: browsers and PHP's stream wrapper both drop the body or re-issue as
# GET. So https://seancheren.com/CalMind/api/index.php answered every sync
# attempt with a redirect to a POST-only endpoint. The status page's own API
# probe is what caught it — it reported 301 and never reached the API at all.
#
# A GET of /CalMind/ still lands on /calmind/ via mod_speling's case
# correction below, which is harmless: browsers follow it, it is the same
# content, and mod_speling deliberately never redirects a POST. Both spellings
# work, so every installed PWA — all of which derive /calmind/api from their
# own origin — keeps working byte for byte.

# The meeting-request link, at the short address people are given (Sean,
# 2026-08-21: "make seancheren.com/request point to seancheren.com/calmind/
# request"). It is CalMind's public page and lives under /calmind; this is the
# apex's alias for it.
#
# A REDIRECT, not an internal rewrite, and that is forced rather than chosen:
# the app builds its API address relative to the page it is on
# (`new URL('api/index.php', location.href)` — apps/app/src/config.ts). Served
# in place at /request the page would ask /api/index.php, which does not
# exist, and a request page that draws a calendar it can never fill is worse
# than a 404. Redirected, it runs at its own path with everything already
# proven there.
#
# 302, not 301: a permanent redirect is cached by browsers past the point of
# recall, and this is a one-line decision he may want back.
#
# NC so /Request works too — mod_speling below only rescues paths that name a
# real FILE, and this one names a rewrite rule. Before the host routing, so
# the subdomains get the same alias: the target is host-relative, so on
# test.seancheren.com it lands on test's own copy.
#
# The target is ABSOLUTE, and https is written out by hand, because NFSN
# terminates TLS in front of Apache and forwards plain http: Apache believes
# every request arrived unencrypted, so a path-only target gets expanded to
# "http://…" and an https visitor is bounced out to plaintext before the
# site's own http->https 301 drags them back. Two extra round trips and one
# of them in the clear, for a link handed to strangers. HTTP_HOST rather than
# a literal so test and dev keep their own hostname.
RewriteRule ^request/?$ https://%{HTTP_HOST}/calmind/request [R=302,L,NC]

# ── Subdomain routing (Sean, 2026-08-20) ────────────────────────────────────
#
# seancheren.com/test/XXXX becomes test.seancheren.com/XXXX, and the same for
# dev. The aliases serve this one docroot — NFSN gives a site a single
# /home/public — so the split is done here by HTTP_HOST rather than by giving
# each subdomain a root of its own.
#
# PROD keeps the apex and no subdomain: it is what every app, device and
# browser points at. test and dev are web-only.
#
# The !^/test/ condition is what stops the rewrite recursing: without it the
# rewritten /test/foo comes back round and becomes /test/test/foo.
RewriteCond %{HTTP_HOST} ^test\. [NC]
RewriteCond %{REQUEST_URI} !^/test/
RewriteRule ^(.*)$ /test/$1 [L]

# The /dev slot is BACK (Sean, 2026-08-23: "deploy a clone from prod to test
# and dev.. make sure test and dev have completely separate accounts and
# data"). It was retired earlier the same day; the difference now is that it
# is a real third instance with its own lib-dev, data-dev and account store,
# not an unowned directory. Same shape as test, one hostname over.
RewriteCond %{HTTP_HOST} ^dev\. [NC]
RewriteCond %{REQUEST_URI} !^/dev/
RewriteRule ^(.*)$ /dev/$1 [L]

# …and the old paths on the APEX are GONE — not moved (Sean, 2026-08-20: "it
# should just stop working"). A 301 would keep every old bookmark and link
# alive at a new address, which is the opposite of retiring a URL. The apex is
# prod and nothing else.
#
# Host-guarded: on test.seancheren.com the internal rewrite above has already
# produced /test/..., and 404ing that would break the subdomain itself.
# Matched on REQUEST_URI, not on the rule pattern, because THIS FILE IS ALSO
# DEPLOYED INTO /home/public/test/ — deploy.sh rsyncs public/ into the mirror,
# so a copy of it runs as the per-directory config for everything under /test/.
# In that copy the per-directory path has already had "test/" stripped, so
# `RewriteRule ^test/(.*)$` matched nothing and the rule looked broken while
# the file it was in demonstrably worked (the CalMind capitalisation 301 above
# kept firing). REQUEST_URI is the whole path in either context.
# Turning this on the first time broke PROD, and the reason is worth keeping:
# the web export was built with baseUrl=/test/calmind, so
# seancheren.com/calmind's index.html loaded its JS from /test/calmind/_expo/…
# — the very path this rule makes vanish. White screen. The rule was not
# wrong; the export was pointing somewhere it had no business pointing.
#
# baseUrl is /calmind for every instance now, which is what the subdomains
# made possible: on test.seancheren.com the internal rewrite above maps
# /calmind to /test/calmind, so ONE export serves all three and no instance
# reaches into another's directory for its own assets.
RewriteCond %{HTTP_HOST} !^(test|dev)\. [NC]
RewriteCond %{REQUEST_URI} ^/(test|dev)/
RewriteRule ^ - [R=404,L]

# ── Case-insensitive paths, site-wide (Sean, 2026-08-20) ────────────────────
#
# "any seancheren.com/xxxxx urls should not be case sensitive". The filesystem
# is case-sensitive, so /AkisBookshelf 404s while /akisbookshelf serves. The
# CalMind rule above solves exactly one path by naming its four capitalisations
# by hand, which does not scale to every app on the site and never covered
# /Chat, /Projects or anything added later.
#
# mod_speling does it generally: on a request that would otherwise 404, it
# looks for a file differing only in case and 301s to the real name.
# CheckCaseOnly keeps it to CASE — without it the module also "corrects"
# misspellings and transpositions, which turns a genuine 404 into a redirect
# to something the visitor did not ask for.
#
# Wrapped in IfModule: NFSN's Apache rejects unknown directives in .htaccess
# with a hard 500, which has taken this whole suite down before (see the note
# about php_flag at the top of this file).
<IfModule mod_speling.c>
  CheckSpelling On
  CheckCaseOnly On
</IfModule>

# ── Nothing an editor or a VCS left behind is ever served ──────────────────
#
# 2026-08-23: https://seancheren.com/.index.html.swp answered 200. A Vim swap
# file, sitting in the web root since April, handing out the account name, the
# internal hostname (seancheren.nfshost.com) and the filesystem path to anyone
# who asked. Found by sweeping the docroot for exactly this class of file
# after Sean asked whether anything else was quietly reachable.
#
# The file is gone, but the CLASS is the thing to close: an editor crash, an
# interrupted rsync or a stray `cp x x.bak` puts another one there, and
# nothing about the deploy would notice. A 404 by pattern costs nothing and
# does not depend on anybody remembering.
#
# .git is in here even though nothing checks out on the server — it is the
# single worst thing that can appear in a docroot, and the rule is free.
<FilesMatch "(^\.|\.(swp|swo|swn|bak|orig|rej|save|old|tmp|log|sql|ini|sh|conf)$|~$)">
  Require all denied
  # …and never cacheable. Denying it at the origin was not enough: the edge
  # kept serving the swap file's old 200 for the bare URL long after the file
  # was gone (a query string bypassed the cache and correctly got 403). A
  # stray file that appears for even a minute must not then be served from a
  # cache for hours. IfModule because this host answers an unknown directive
  # with a hard 500 for the whole site.
  <IfModule mod_headers.c>
    Header always set Cache-Control "no-store, no-cache, must-revalidate, max-age=0"
  </IfModule>
</FilesMatch>
# …except the one dotted path that MUST be served: ACME lives there, and a
# blocked .well-known means the certificate stops renewing and the whole site
# goes to a TLS warning some weeks later. That failure would arrive long after
# this change and look nothing like it.
<FilesMatch "^\.well-known">
  Require all granted
</FilesMatch>
